+# Pickup reminders
+
+For whoever changes how members are reminded of a pickup, or proposes sending reminders by email to save the SMS
+cost. The day before pickup, larder texts each member that their pantry order is ready. Reminders go by SMS because
+from April to June 2025 they went by email, and missed pickups rose from 6% to 13% of pantry orders.
+
+## Sending
+
+`remind` in `larder/reminders.py` sends the member one message the day before their pickup slot:
+"Your pantry order is ready for pickup" followed by the slot.
+
+## Channel
+
+The channel is `CHANNEL` in `larder/reminders.py`, `"sms"`: the message goes to the member's phone. The code can
+send it by email instead, and that is the change volunteers keep proposing, since SMS costs the pantry about 40 euros
+a month and email costs nothing.
+
+Email was tried from April to June 2025. The email provider reported that 34% of the reminder emails were never
+opened, and missed pickups went from 6% of pantry orders to 13%. Each missed pickup is a bag volunteers packed for
+nothing, and the fresh food in it goes in the bin by closing time. Many members share one phone across the family and
+never check email; a text they see. Reminders went back to SMS on July 1, 2025, and missed pickups were back to 6% by
+August.
+
+The question came up a third time in one year in issue 52 (`notes/issue-52.md`), and the maintainer kept SMS: the
+40 euros a month was weighed against the missed pickups and food above, and lost.
+
+## Shaped by
+
+- No decision record: the channel is one constant in one module, below the bar for a record, so its reason lives on
+  this page.
